  "smbd_deltas": "the smbd map replaces the nes warp zone (real cols 177-190) with an enclosed coin room: brick platforms, three question blocks, eleven coins, a coin brick, a power-up brick and its own sideways exit pipe, reached only by riding the second lift onto the roof and dropping through the hole at cols 187-190. that room is what this file builds. the smbd map also trims the closing staircase to eight columns and seven blocks and pulls the flag one column closer. everything else matches the nes map cell for cell",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.mariouniverse.com/wp-content/img/maps/gbc/smbd/ch/1-2.png",
    "https://www.mariouniverse.com/wp-content/img/maps/nes/smb/1-2.png"
  ],
  "confidence_notes": "every cell of this file was read off the smbd challenge-mode map rip by hashing each 16x16 cell of the image and labelling the distinct tiles, then cross-checked cell by cell against the nes map rip, which agrees on the whole underground run. the start segment (castle, pipes, ground 0-23) comes from the nes map, which is the only rip that draws it. enemies are placed at the column the smbd rip draws them; a sprite the rip straddles across two columns is put on one of them. piranha plants are not drawn in the smbd rip but stand on the same pipes in the gbc game, so they are taken from the nes rip. the two vertical lifts are ours in size and speed (a two-block deck bouncing at one pixel a frame, one deck per pit, the engine having two lift slots); their pits, the open roof above them and the roof walk they lead to are measured. challenge-mode-only content (red coins, the yoshi egg) is recorded under smbd and not built."
